     SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

     Widget colours, light theme.

     ── Why the tonal palette and not theme attributes ────────────────────────

     A widget's RemoteViews are inflated in the LAUNCHER's process against the
     launcher's theme, so `?android:attr/colorSurface` resolves to whatever the
     host happens to use — which on Pixel Launcher is not the same surface a
     Settings page would give you, and is not guaranteed to track the wallpaper.

     @android:color/system_* IS the wallpaper-derived Material You palette, is
     public since Android 12 (core/res/res/values/public-final.xml:3123 and
     neighbours), and is regenerated by the system whenever the user changes
     wallpaper or accent. That is what makes the widget belong on the home screen
     instead of merely surviving there.

     Day/night is handled by the values-night/ copy of this file rather than by
     an attribute, because the resource resolution happens in the host at apply
     time — RemoteViews.java:8384-8398 inflates through a context wrapper that
     carries the host's configuration, so the -night qualifier is honoured.

     ── The two states ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     A one-tap toggle has to say which way it is pointing from across a 13" desk,
     so the two states differ in FILL WEIGHT and not only in hue:

       Daily (resting) — quiet tinted surface, accent glyph on a light disc.
           Reads as "off", the way an inactive Quick Settings tile does.
       Game (active)   — the card ITSELF is filled with accent1 and the text and
           glyph invert onto it. Reads as "on" at any distance.

     ⚠️ The revision before this one tinted only the icon disc and left both
     cards pale — neutral1_100 against accent1_100, which are a few percent apart
     in luminance. Photographed on the home screen they were indistinguishable
     and the only real difference was one word, which is the exact failure the
     revision before THAT was meant to fix. Contrast between the two states has
     to come from the largest element on the widget, not the smallest.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Daily (resting) -->
    <color name="widget_bg">@android:color/system_accent2_50</color>
    <color name="widget_icon_bg">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>
    <color name="widget_title">@android:color/system_neutral1_900</color>
    <color name="widget_detail">@android:color/system_neutral2_700</color>
    <color name="widget_icon">@android:color/system_accent1_700</color>

    <!-- Game (active) -->
    <color name="widget_bg_active">@android:color/system_accent1_600</color>
    <color name="widget_icon_bg_active">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>
    <color name="widget_title_active">@android:color/system_accent1_50</color>
    <color name="widget_detail_active">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>
    <color name="widget_icon_active">@android:color/system_accent1_700</color>
</resources>
